                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Accessibility](/docs/guide/accessibility)") }
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("The same component, three ways", id: "the-same-component-three-ways").h2

        Markdown("React:")

        CodeBlock(#"function Counter({ start = 0, onChange }) {
  const [n, setN] = useState(start);
  const even = useMemo(() => n % 2 === 0, [n]);
  return (
    <div className="row">
      <button onClick={() => { setN(n + 1); onChange?.(n + 1); }}>+</button>
      <span>{n}</span>
      {even && <span className="badge">even</span>}
    </div>
  );
}"#, title: "jsx")

        Markdown("WebFluent:")

        CodeBlock(#"component Counter(start: Number = 0) {
    event change(value: Number)
    state n = start
    derived even = n % 2 == 0
    Row(gap: .sm, align: .center) {
        Button("+") { on click { n = n + 1  emit change(n) } }
        Text("{n}")
        if even { Badge("even").info }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("What changed: no hooks and no dependency arrays — `derived` knows what it read. No setter — you assign. No `className` — `Badge` is designed, and `.info` is a flag the compiler checks. The event is declared, so a caller handling `on chnage` is an error, not a handler that never runs.")

        Heading("What is genuinely different", id: "what-is-genuinely-different").h2

        Markdown("**It is a compiler, not a library.** There is no virtual DOM and no re-render of a component. Each text, attribute or style that reads a state subscribes to it and updates alone. A component's body runs once, when it is placed.\n\n**The markup is typed.** A built-in's props, flags, events and slots are known to the compiler, and so are yours. `Button(\"Save\").primray` does not build. There is no \"any prop goes through to the DOM\" — an unknown named argument becomes an HTML attribute with a warning, unless it is `aria-*`, `data-*` or a global attribute.\n\n**Pages own their routes.** There is no route table or file-system routing: each `page` names its `path:` and the router is built from them, ordered by specificity.\n\n**No imports.** Every file under `src/` is one program. A component declared anywhere is usable everywhere; two declarations with one name are an error.\n\n**Styling lives on the element.** A `style \{ \}` block is real CSS scoped to that element; `$token` reads a design token. There is no separate CSS-in-JS runtime — static rules are compiled into the stylesheet.\n\n**The build is the site.** Pre-rendering, code splitting, precompression, image resizing, a sitemap, Open Graph, a CSP — all from `wf build`, with no plugins to choose.\n\n**Safe by construction.** Text is always text; the only way to inject markup is `Unsafe.Html(…)`, which the build flags every time. Inline handlers and `javascript:` URLs do not compile. See [Security](/docs/guide/security).")

        Heading("Things you might look for", id: "things-you-might-look-for").h2

        Markdown("- **npm packages.** There is no package manager for WebFluent code. A JavaScript library is used through `external` — a typed declaration of what you call — and `Host`, which gives it a DOM node with a lifetime. [JavaScript interop](/docs/guide/interop) shows a chart library end to end.\n- **Server components / SSR per request.** Not here: a WebFluent site is static files plus a browser runtime, served by any host, talking to your API. For HTML generated on a server — emails, invoices, fragments — see [Server rendering](/docs/guide/server-rendering).\n- **Using WebFluent inside an existing React or Vue app.** Publish components as custom elements and place the tags: [Custom elements](/docs/guide/interop#publishing-your-components-as-custom-elements).\n- **Coming from HTMX.** The closest thing to `hx-get` is a `resource` whose URL reads state, and to `hx-post` an action that `await`s an `api` endpoint. The page stays static HTML until its script arrives, then updates in place.")
    }
}
